.TH "watch" 1G "5 October 2026" GINI "gRouter Commands"

.SH NAME
watch \- record what happens to each packet at the router

.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
watch on | off
watch show
watch dump [since_seq]
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION

When watch is on, the router writes one record for every packet it handles: where it came in,
where it went, and if it did not get through, what stopped it. The Router Lab's packet
visualizer and its Packets tab are drawn from these records, and you can read them here.

Watch is OFF when the router starts and costs nothing while off. The Router Lab turns it on while
the visualizer is showing and off again when you close the Lab.

.B on
and
.B off
start and stop recording.
.B show
says whether it is on and how many packets have been recorded since the router started.
.B dump
prints the records, oldest first. The router keeps the last 512; give the sequence number you
last saw to get only the newer ones.

.SH OUTPUT

dump prints a header line, then one line per packet:
.RS
.nf
WATCH on|off next_seq router_ms
W seq ms fate in_if out_if module module_type src dst proto ttl len sport dport
.fi
.RE

fate is one letter:
.RS
.nf
F  forwarded out interface out_if
D  dropped by pipeline module number module, of type module_type (see gpipe list)
N  no route to the destination
T  TTL expired here (the router sends back ICMP time exceeded)
L  addressed to the router itself (a ping to one of its interfaces)
A  an ARP arrived; module_type says answered, learned or ignored
S  sent by the router itself: its own ARP requests and replies, and the ICMP it
   originates (echo replies, time exceeded, unreachable); in_if is -1
.fi
.RE

in_if and out_if are interface numbers (1 is tun1); -1 means none. proto is the IP protocol
(1 ICMP, 6 TCP, 17 UDP), or 2054 for ARP. For TCP and UDP, sport and dport are the ports; for ICMP
they hold the type and code (8 0 is an echo request, 0 0 an echo reply, 11 0 time exceeded); for
ARP sport is 1 for a request and 2 for a reply. ms is the router's clock in milliseconds, good for
spacing between packets, not for the time of day.

If the first seq is more than one past the since_seq you asked for, packets came and went faster
than the ring could hold them between two dumps.

.SH EXAMPLES

Turn watch on, ping across the router from a machine, then read what happened:
.RS
.nf
watch on
watch dump
watch off
.fi
.RE

.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR gpipe (1G),
.BR ifstat (1G),
.BR route (1G)
